---
name: conformance-unit-literal-pattern
---

# `case () =>` matches the unit value

`()` is the unit value, so a literal pattern on it must hold. jvm (real Scala), js and int all
answer `UNIT`; the two self-hosted lanes answered `OTHER`.

**It was not a mismatch — the arm was silently DROPPED.** Lowering the repro through the reference
front produced `(def viaLiteral (lam 1 (let ((local 0)) (lit (str "OTHER")))))`: no test, no arms,
the whole `match` collapsed into "bind the scrutinee, return the default". `()` in PATTERN position
went to the empty-tuple path and disappeared, so the arm never existed to fail.

The last row is the one that keeps the fix honest: a real one-element tuple pattern must still work,
because the fix separates `()` from the tuple path that used to swallow it.

```scalascript
def viaLiteral(x: Any): String = x match
  case () => "UNIT"
  case _  => "OTHER"

def firstOf(p: (Int, Int)): Int = p match
  case (a, _) => a

def main() =
  println(viaLiteral(()))
  println(viaLiteral(7))
  println(viaLiteral("s"))
  // the value of a `println` IS unit — the shape the defect was found with
  println(viaLiteral(println("")))
  // tuple patterns must be undisturbed by separating `()` from them
  println(firstOf((4, 5)))
```
